+
+# Throttle login attempts per client IP
+
+## Context
+
+`POST /login` answered an unlimited number of submissions. A script could
+walk a credential-stuffing list against one account at whatever rate the
+process could serve, and every guess was processed exactly like a single
+mistyped password. Nothing slowed an attacker down.
+
+## Decision
+
+`POST /login` runs behind a rate limiter (`app/Http/Middleware/ThrottleLogin.ts`,
+wired into the route in `routes/auth.ts`): 5 submissions per client per
+minute, after which further submissions in that window get 429 with a
+`Retry-After` header.
+
+- **Every submission counts**, a correct password included. A success that
+  cleared the counter would hand back the attempts it just spent — finding
+  a working password is the attacker's goal, not a reason to stop counting.
+- **"Client" is the connection's peer address.** `trustProxy` stays off: no
+  proxy fronts this app, so `X-Forwarded-For` and friends are attacker-typed
+  strings and trusting them would let one client mint an identity per request.
+  Put the app behind a proxy and this decision has to be revisited together
+  with that deployment change.
+- **Unresolvable peer address fails closed** — those requests share one
+  bucket and are throttled together rather than waved through.
+- **Counters are in memory.** One process serves the app and a one-minute
+  window is not worth persisting; a restart clearing counters costs an
+  attacker more than it costs us.
+- Only the submission is throttled. Rendering the form, registration, and
+  every other route are untouched, so a throttled client can still load
+  `/login` and read the site.
+
+## Consequences
+
+The form is no longer a free oracle: brute force drops to 5 guesses per
+minute per address, which makes an online attack impractical without
+changing anything about how a legitimate person signs in.
+
+A shared address (office NAT, a mobile carrier) shares the budget, so a
+crowd behind one IP can throttle each other. Five per minute leaves enough
+headroom that this is unlikely to be noticed in practice.
+
+Because counters are per process and in memory, this stops being a real
+limit the moment the app runs on more than one instance — a shared store
+(Redis) is the follow-up if we scale out.
